.TE
.PP
The library needs C++23: g++ 15 or later, clang 23, or the AppleClang of
Xcode 16 or 26.
Every part builds on each of them, and \f[CR]cbor::schema\f[R] is one
class for all of them.
With g++ 16 and \f[CR]\-std=c++26 \-freflection\f[R], \f[CR]structs\f[R]
reads the members by reflection and \f[CR]schema\f[R] checks each
description against the members.
On every other compiler, \f[CR]structs\f[R] reads the members through
Boost.PFR (section 5).
Every example includes \f[CR]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R].
The reference with examples that run in the browser:
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/.
A comment shows the CBOR in \f[CR]bytes\f[R] as text, in the diagnostic
notation of RFC 8949, which looks like JSON.
.SS 1. Errors
A call that reads CBOR returns a
\f[CR]std::expected<T, cbor::error>\f[R].
It holds the value or the error.
Bad input never throws.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dquser\(dq: {\(dqid\(dq: 42}}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const id = cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.id\(dq, std::int64_t>(bytes);
\f[B]if\f[R] (id)
    use(*id);                                           \f[I]// 42\f[R]
\f[B]else\f[R]
    report(std::error_code{id.error()}.message());

\f[B]auto\f[R] const age = cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.age\(dq, std::int64_t>(bytes);
age.error() == cbor::error::key_not_found;              \f[I]// true\f[R]
std::error_code{age.error()}.message();                 \f[I]// \(dqkey not found\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
These are the errors that a program meets most often:
.PP
