.PP
A wrong use of the library does not compile, or it throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
Do not catch \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: it is a fault of the program,
not of the input.
.SS 2. Results that point into the bytes
A text or a byte string comes back as a view into the bytes, with no
copy.
The view sits in a \f[CR]cbor::ref\f[R].
The \f[CR]ref\f[R] holds the bytes, so the view stays valid while the
\f[CR]ref\f[R] lives.
.PP
Bytes go into the library in three forms, and no form copies them:
.IP \(bu 2
An rvalue \f[CR]std::string\f[R] is moved into storage that the library
owns.
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R] is read in place, and
every result keeps it alive.
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<void const>\f[R] and a
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] beside it are read in place.
The owner keeps the bytes of the view alive.
The library does not check that it holds them.
A \f[CR]std::string\f[R] lvalue beside an owner does not compile.
.PP
A read that gives a number also takes a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R],
because its result points into nothing.
A read with \f[CR]std::string\f[R] or \f[CR]std::vector<std::byte>\f[R]
as the type also takes a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R].
It copies the text or the bytes of the value into the result.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqname\(dq: \(dqann\(dq}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const name = cbor::at_path<\(dq$.name\(dq, std::string_view>(owner);
\f[B]if\f[R] (name) {
    std::string_view const text = **name;   \f[I]// \(dqann\(dq\f[R]
    std::string const copy{text};           \f[I]// a copy of your own\f[R]
    *name == \(dqann\(dq;                         \f[I]// true\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]*name\f[R] is the \f[CR]ref\f[R], and \f[CR]**name\f[R] is the
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R].
\f[CR]text\f[R] stays valid after \f[CR]owner\f[R] ends, because
\f[CR]name\f[R] holds the bytes.
Keep a result in a variable: \f[CR]*\f[R] and \f[CR]\->\f[R] on a
temporary \f[CR]ref\f[R] do not compile.
A \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R], a result of \f[CR]structs\f[R] and an accessor
of \f[CR]schema\f[R] hold the bytes in the same way.
.SS 3. Threads
Many threads can read the same bytes at the same time, while nothing
writes them.
A \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] and every result from it belong to the thread
that made them, so each thread makes its own lazy.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// owner: a std::shared_ptr<std::string const> with {\(dqid\(dq: 42}\f[R]
std::thread first([&owner] { use(cbor::at_path<\(dq$.id\(dq, std::int64_t>(*owner)); });
std::thread second([&owner] {
    \f[B]if\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const doc = cbor::lazy::view(owner))   \f[I]// the lazy of this thread\f[R]
        use(doc\->at_path<\(dq$.id\(dq, std::int64_t>());
});
first.join();
second.join();
.EE
.PP
The library starts no thread and holds no lock.
.SS 4. Limits
A limit stops input that holds a string or a container that is too
large.
A limit is a constant of the build: a macro sets it, and
\f[CR]cbor::limits\f[R] gives its value.
.PP
