.PP
Every translation unit of one program uses the same values.
A limit that the build does not set gets its default.
A check whose limit is not set is not in the program.
The library does not bound the size of the input: the program bounds it
before it calls the library.
.PP
The error comes at the read that meets the value.
A count in the input reserves no memory: memory grows only with the
values that a call reads.
.PP
Nesting has no fixed limit.
A reader that recurses stops where the stack of its thread ends: a call
that comes within 16 KiB of the end (32 KiB on Windows or with a
sanitizer) returns \f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R].
Each thread reads the bounds of its own stack once, at its first check:
from the system on Linux (glibc and musl), macOS and Windows, and from
the linker on WebAssembly without threads and on picolibc.
Another target does not compile.
A frame outside these bounds, such as the stack of a fiber or of a
signal handler, is not checked.
The first check of a thread does not run in a signal handler.
On Linux, a change of \f[CR]RLIMIT_STACK\f[R] after the first check, and
a mapping that a program places with \f[CR]MAP_FIXED\f[R] below the
stack of the main thread, are outside the bound.
On picolibc, a stack smaller than the reserve refuses every nested item.
\f[CR]item_size\f[R], \f[CR]sequence_reader\f[R] and a path of child
segments do not recurse and read any depth.
A deep value of a user type (\f[CR]structs\f[R], \f[CR]schema\f[R]) is
destroyed and copied by its own recursion: a thread with a smaller stack
than the one that decoded it can overflow there.
.SS 5. Boost.PFR
CMake looks for Boost 1.84 or later on the system and uses it.
Else the library uses its own copy of Boost.PFR 1.92.0 in
\f[CR]third_party/boost_pfr\f[R], the files of boostorg/pfr, under the
Boost Software License 1.0.
An install then places this copy in
\f[CR]include/mbedded\-cbor/boost_pfr\f[R], and the CMake package and
the pkg\-config file add it to the include path.
The configure output says which of the two the build takes.
Where a build has Boost.PFR and no reflection, \f[CR]schema\f[R] also
checks that the description of an aggregate lists as many members as
Boost.PFR counts.
.PP
Every translation unit of a program must see the same Boost.PFR.
A program that also uses another Boost.PFR of its own breaks the
one\-definition rule.
An older Boost.PFR that comes first on the include path stops the build
with an error that names version 1.84.
.PP
More: every error value, the limits in detail, memory, and a lazy that
moves to another thread are in advanced/basics.md.
